  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe latest radar imagery and surface observations reveal pre-\nfrontal activity exiting east of the area early this morning,\nwhile more widespread rain approaches from the west associated \nwith a cold front. Given the unfavorable timing of the front\nthis morning, the threat for any stronger showers or storms\nacross our area appears low. A reinforcing cold front/secondary\ntrough will arrive from the northwest later this afternoon and \nevening, bringing another round of rain and colder air across \nthe region, characterized by 850 mb temperatures falling as low\nas -6 to -7 degrees C by Saturday morning. A period of lake\neffect rain is likely to develop downwind of Lakes Huron and\nErie, especially later tonight into Saturday morning between the\nI-71/I-77 corridors. \n\nWinds will remain elevated for much of the day in the 25 to 30\nmph range, though will be most gusty (up to 40 mph) with the \nfrontal passage this morning, and with the secondary trough \nlater this afternoon and evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will gradually build from the west on Saturday, \nsetting the stage for a prolonged period of generally quiet\nweather through the upcoming week. The cold air mass in place\nwith the building high should result in efficient radiative\ncooling, especially Saturday night into Sunday morning where\ntemperatures are expected to fall into the low 30s or even upper\n20s in some spots. At the minimum, a widespread frost is\nexpected across much of the area and headlines will be needed.\nFrost conditions may return again Sunday night into Monday as\nhigh pressure continues to influence the weather, though lows\nmay be slightly more moderated in the mid to upper 30s with\nincreasing warm air advection.",
